{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-71035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-3:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6+10t \\\\ -2+3t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d544dca39e67026fca4e2f5913c8e174cb142d54c7a9af77d54db6a8e4ea1aa"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y+10}{6}=\\frac{z+3}{-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y-10}{6}=\\frac{z-3}{-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{y+6}{10}=\\frac{z-2}{3},\\,x=3"] ["math" "\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3"] ["text" "None of the listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t \\\\ y=5-t \\\\ z=-7+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-2t \\\\ 5-t \\\\ -7+t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,5,-7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-2,-1,1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d544dca39e67026fca4e2f5913c8e174cb142d54c7a9af77d54db6a8e4ea1aa"} {"question" "q-127401" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127401" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z-7}{1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+2}{-1}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+2}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z-1}{-7}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127401" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-2t \\\\ y=-5t \\\\ z=1-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3-2t \\\\ -5t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ -5t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(3,0,1)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-2,-5,-1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y-0}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d544dca39e67026fca4e2f5913c8e174cb142d54c7a9af77d54db6a8e4ea1aa"} {"question" "q-127410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+2}{3}=\\frac{y+5}{1}=\\frac{z+1}{-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+2}{3}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z+1}{1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{-1}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z+1}{-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z+1}{-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/assets/1647eb5cca32c6fdc99e23507db151a8299b29bfa70ebfbaa941e17fe4638586.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,4,5)$ and $Q(7,-1,2).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨7,-1,2⟩-⟨-1,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-5,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d544dca39e67026fca4e2f5913c8e174cb142d54c7a9af77d54db6a8e4ea1aa"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+1}{-8}=\\frac{y-4}{-3}=\\frac{z-5}{-5}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+1}{-8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y+4}{-5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/assets/8722b93655e069e2f35147b572c16ed7af408a86f8a88597c79aea8fe59e9c4d.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the line passes through the points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-3,3⟩-⟨2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3.$ For example, our two points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3)$ both have $z$-coordinates of $z=3.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ part and instead write $z=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d544dca39e67026fca4e2f5913c8e174cb142d54c7a9af77d54db6a8e4ea1aa"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{4},\\,z=3"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{-4},\\,z=-3"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{4},\\,z=3"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{-4},\\,z=-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "8944381408f3a84e49feddf0d5436868afc1fd27adc12d06cbd83ee92ab9165a"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2006 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
